Pipe shells' rounded corners where the intersection of the two sides
breaks up (mitcad#1564). Applies after 1663.

A rounded corner (BRepFill_Round) turns each side's bound about the axis
through the corner square to both of its tangents, where the bound does
not reach the bisector plane; where it reaches past the plane, the two
sides overlap and BRepFill_TrimShellCorner trims them against each other
along their intersection. The two sides of one section touch where the
bounds cross the plane (on that axis), and at a small corner they meet at
a shallow angle all along the overlap: there the intersection of
approximated sides comes in pieces, the trim finds no loop, and
BRepFill_Sweep turned the whole bounds about the corner over the
untrimmed sides (a shell through itself); where that was not a valid
shell, MitCad's pipes carried the section unturned through the corner,
leaning by the corner's angle.

When that trim fails for a rounded corner of a closed section, each side
is now cut where it crosses the bisector plane, as 1663 cuts a mitre,
only where its bound reaches past the plane: from its bound, the first
crossing along the path. Where the pieces are straight near the corner
the two sides are mirror images in the plane and their intersection lies
in it; elsewhere the two cuts lie within the bend over the overlap's
depth of each other. The loop round the section is broken where the
bounds cross the plane (one vertex where both sides' do: on the axis the
round turns about) and where the U-edges that reach past the plane cross
it; each edge between two breaks past the plane lies on one face of each
side, runs through the middles of the two cuts and has each side's cut
as its curve on that face. Each bound is kept where it does not reach the
plane, as pieces with the bound's curve, which BRepFill_Sweep's round
then turns about the corner as it turns the pieces of the trim along the
intersection: their ends at the breaks lie on the axis, and its turns
close there in degenerate edges. The U-edges that reach past the plane
end at its breaks. Refused, as 1663, where the two cuts lie further apart
than 5e-4 of the section's extent, where the bounds do not cross the
plane, and where a bound crosses it at a U-edge.

The overlap inside a corner of 12 to 28 degrees reaches up to r tan(14)
along the curved sides, where the two sides' intersection lies off the
plane by their bend over that depth (1664's prisms keep a mitre's sides
straight there; a round's are the sides themselves). Each point of the
cut, and each U-edge's break, is therefore moved from the plane to where
the first side's line there (the U-edge's own side's) meets the other
side, by Newton's method from the two cuts' points; where it does not
converge (the sides meet nearly tangentially, near the bounds' crossings,
where the two cuts nearly meet anyway) the point stays in the middle of
the two cuts.

BRepFill_Sweep's round (Filling) gives the second side's end its curve
on the turn by the first side's end turned, the parameters mapped
linearly. Within twice the sweep's tolerance (the two sides' ends are
each approximated within it) the edge's tolerance now covers how far its
curve lies from the turn's (it was the turn's 1e-7 mm); further off, the
piece is not the turned end (8 mm off, a diameter, where the trim along
the intersection kept the other half of a side's end) and no turn is
made, so that the try fails instead of making a solid through itself. A
row's turn reuses the turn's edge the previous row made at their common
end with that row's angle, so that the two meet there (each row's angle
is measured at its own farthest point and they differ slightly).
Oriente, which orients a turn's edges by the bound of the turn's surface
they lie at, took a degenerated edge at the turn's first end for one at
its last where the surface is trimmed on a periodic profile (its bounds
moved into the first period, 2 pi to 0, while the curves on it keep the
profile's parameters) and reversed it, so the turn's wire did not close
on its face: where an iso-line lies at neither bound, on a surface whose
basis is periodic that way (a surface trimmed in a direction is not
periodic that way itself), it is at the bound it is nearer to over the
period.
Where the turn
of a point next to the axis between two vertices that lie apart (the
trim along the intersection leaves the two sides' ends so) cannot be made
an edge and its arc is no longer than the distance between them, it is a
degenerated edge as for a point on the axis (BRepLib_MakeEdge's
StdFail_NotDone stopped the sweep); and BuildFace's test of whether a
turn is planar no longer asks edges that make no wire for their wire.

With MITCAD_TRACE_PIPE_CORNERS set, BRepFill_Sweep::PerformCorner and
the trim write what each corner does on the standard error: its angle and
kind, whether the trim along the intersection was done, where the cut
along the bisector plane was refused (the line of the file) or how far
apart its two sides lay, how many pieces of the two sides' ends each row
turns and whether each turn was built.

MitCad's geometry.pipe_square_section checks an 8 mm pipe with rounded
corners along the intersection curve of a sine wave drawn as ten lines
on XZ and an arc on XY, and along two B-spline pieces meeting at 3
degrees: rounded at every corner, square to the path within 0.01 degrees
at 20 points, its volume within 0.1 % of the legs' less their overlaps
plus the turns'.

